  Root cause: _ensure_test_file() (agents/implementation/consumer_migration.py:197) generates the stub with:

  assert "{new_field}" in event
  assert "{old_field}" not in event

  In this case old_field = "customer_id" and new_field = "account_id". The stub should assert "customer_id" n
  ot in event. But the current file has "account_id" not in event — meaning a previous agent run already ran
  _migrate_test_file() on the auto-generated stub, blindly replacing "customer_id" with "account_id" on line
  6 as well, turning the sentinel assertion into nonsense.

  The fix: The migration agent should not re-migrate the auto-generated sentinel line (assert "{old_field}" n
  ot in event). That line is a proof assertion — it must keep the old field name to prove it's gone. The rege
  x substitution is too broad and clobbers it.

  The cleanest fix is in _ensure_test_file: the stub's old_field not in event line uses a negative assertion
  about the old field. When _migrate_test_file runs over this file, the not in event check for old_field shou
  ld be left alone because it is already the correct post-migration assertion. The issue is _migrate_python_s
  ource replaces all occurrences of old_field, including this proof-assertion line.

  The fix needs to be in _migrate_test_file — it should skip lines where the pattern is a negative assertion
  about old_field (i.e., assert "old_field" not in ...). Alternatively, the simpler and more robust fix is: d
  on't call _migrate_test_file on the auto-generated sentinel test — but the agent can't know which test is t
  he sentinel.

  The real root is that _migrate_python_source replaces quoted string literals inside assert statements, whic
  h is wrong for assertion lines that are proving the field is absent. The assert "customer_id" not in event
  line is not a use of customer_id — it's an assertion that it's been removed. It must survive unchanged.

  The right fix: in _migrate_test_file, skip replacement for lines that match the pattern assert "<old_field>
  " not in. These are proof-of-removal assertions, not field usages.
